        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2>Personal and fan heaters</h2>
  <p>
    Small personal heaters and conventional fan heaters use a heating element and fan to push warm
    air into the room.
  </p>
  <p>
    Their main strength is speed. They can be practical when a small study, bedroom or home office
    needs warmth for a limited period. The moving air can make the effect noticeable quickly, even
    before the whole room becomes warm.
  </p>
  <p>
    The compromise is noise. The fan must continue operating to distribute heat, and the effect can
    disappear soon after the heater is turned off. Small models are also easy to expect too much
    from. A unit intended for close personal heating is not a substitute for a properly sized room
    heater.
  </p>
  <p>A fan heater makes more sense when:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>warmth is needed quickly</li>
    <li>the room is small and enclosed</li>
    <li>use is occasional</li>
    <li>fan noise is acceptable</li>
  </ul>
  <p>It is less convincing for large living rooms, overnight use or spaces with persistent draughts.</p>

  <h2>Ceramic heaters</h2>
  <p>
    Ceramic heaters are also fan-assisted resistance heaters, but they use a ceramic heating
    element. Many include oscillation, timers, thermostats, multiple heat settings and tip-over
    protection.
  </p>
  <p>
    They are often presented as fundamentally cheaper to run than ordinary fan heaters. That
    comparison needs care.
  </p>
  <p>
    Portable fan, ceramic, oil column and panel heaters generally create heat through electrical
    resistance. When two heaters have the same rated power and both run continuously at full
    output, their electricity use will be broadly similar. The meaningful differences are how they
    deliver the heat, how well their controls regulate the room and whether the heater is suitable
    for the space.
  </p>
  <p>
    A ceramic heater may be useful where quick warm-air circulation and thermostat control are
    priorities. It does not overcome an undersized appliance, a large open room or significant heat
    loss.
  </p>

  <h2>Oil column heaters</h2>
  <p>
    An oil column heater warms sealed oil inside the appliance. Heat moves through the metal columns
    and then into the room through a combination of convection and low-temperature radiant heat.
  </p>
  <p>The oil is not consumed and does not need topping up.</p>
  <p>
    These heaters are popular in <a href="/categories/bedroom/">bedrooms</a> and quiet living spaces
    because they operate without a fan. They provide gradual, steady warmth and can maintain an
    enclosed room once it is comfortable.
  </p>
  <p>
    The trade-off is response time. An oil column heater can take longer to warm itself and the
    surrounding air, making it less suitable when immediate heat is the priority. It is also heavier
    and bulkier than many fan heaters.
  </p>
  <p>
    Silent operation should not be confused with suitability for unattended or overnight use.
    Follow the manufacturer’s instructions and turn heaters off before leaving the room, leaving the
    home or going to sleep unless the product instructions explicitly state otherwise.
  </p>

  <h2>Panel heaters</h2>
  <p>
    Panel heaters are slim electric resistance heaters designed to provide convection heat.
    Depending on the model, they may be wall-mounted or used as portable floor units with suitable
    feet or castors.
  </p>
  <p>
    Their narrow profile can suit bedrooms, hallways, home offices and other areas where floor space
    matters. They are generally quiet because most rely on natural convection rather than a fan.
  </p>
  <p>
    Panel heaters tend to work best as steady background heating in an enclosed, reasonably
    insulated room. A slim appearance does not mean a small electricity demand, and a low-profile
    panel may still be a high-wattage appliance.
  </p>
  <p>Check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>whether wall mounting is permitted and suitable</li>
    <li>required clearances</li>
    <li>whether mounting hardware is included</li>
    <li>power-point position</li>
    <li>thermostat and timer controls</li>
    <li>the manufacturer’s suggested room size</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Renters should confirm whether permanent wall mounting is allowed before installation.</p>

  <h2>Radiant heaters</h2>
  <p>
    Radiant heaters direct heat towards people and objects rather than relying entirely on warming
    all the room’s air.
  </p>
  <p>
    This can make them useful for short-term spot heating in a workshop, garage or defined seating
    area. Heat is felt quickly when the user is within the effective zone.
  </p>
  <p>
    The limitation is coverage. Move outside that zone and the room may still feel cold. Open glowing
    elements also require careful placement and clearance.
  </p>
  <p>
    Radiant heaters should not be treated as indoor versions of outdoor patio heaters. Never bring
    an outdoor gas heater, barbecue or other outdoor combustion appliance inside.
  </p>

  <h2>Electric fireplaces and faux fireplaces</h2>
  <p>
    Electric fireplaces combine a flame-effect display with an electric heater. They may be
    freestanding, wall-mounted, built into cabinetry or installed as part of a media wall.
  </p>
  <p>
    For many households, the visual effect is a large part of the appeal. The heating component
    commonly operates like another fan or convection heater, so compare its actual heating capacity
    and controls rather than assuming the fireplace format provides stronger heat.
  </p>
  <p>Before buying, check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>whether the flame effect can operate without heat</li>
    <li>the heater’s rated output</li>
    <li>ventilation and clearance requirements</li>
    <li>power-point access</li>
    <li>wall-mounting or recess dimensions</li>
    <li>servicing access</li>
    <li>clearances from televisions, cabinetry and furnishings</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    An electric fireplace can be a useful supplementary heater, but it should not be chosen as the
    primary heat source for a large room without checking whether its heating output suits the
    space.
  </p>

  <h2>Electric blankets and heated throws</h2>
  <p>
    Electric blankets and heated throws approach the problem differently: they warm the bed or
    person rather than the room.
  </p>
  <p>
    An electric blanket is designed for use with a bed in the configuration specified by its
    manufacturer. A heated throw is designed to sit over a person while they are awake. They should
    not be treated as interchangeable unless the product instructions specifically permit that use.
  </p>
  <p>Useful features may include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>separate controls for each side of a bed</li>
    <li>automatic shut-off</li>
    <li>adjustable heat levels</li>
    <li>detachable controllers for washing</li>
    <li>clearly marked zones</li>
    <li>overheat protection</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Inspect electric bedding before each winter. Do not use it if the fabric is worn, cords are
    damaged, elements are exposed, controllers are cracked or there are scorch marks. Store an
    electric blanket rolled rather than tightly folded, and follow current product-safety advice
    about warming the bed and switching it off before sleeping.
  </p>

  <h2>Reverse-cycle air conditioning</h2>
  <p>
    A reverse-cycle air conditioner is a heat pump. Instead of creating all its heat through
    electrical resistance, it transfers heat between the outdoor and indoor air.
  </p>
  <p>
    That allows the same system to cool the home in summer and heat it in winter. It also makes
    reverse-cycle air conditioning substantially more efficient for regular room heating than
    standard portable resistance heaters.
  </p>
  <p>Common arrangements include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>a single wall-mounted split system</li>
    <li>multiple indoor heads connected to outdoor equipment</li>
    <li>a ducted reverse-cycle system</li>
    <li>window or wall units in smaller spaces</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Correct sizing matters. An undersized system may run continuously without making the space
    comfortable. An oversized system can cycle poorly and may not control temperature as
    effectively as expected.
  </p>
  <p>
    Look at the Zoned Energy Rating Label for the climate zone relevant to where you live. A unit’s
    performance can differ between hot, average and cold zones, so a headline star rating without
    climate context does not tell the whole story.
  </p>
  <p>
    Installation also requires planning. The indoor and outdoor unit positions affect airflow,
    noise, drainage, appearance and servicing access. Refrigerant work must be completed by an
    appropriately licensed technician, and electrical work may also be required.
  </p>
  <p>Regular filter cleaning and servicing help the system continue operating effectively.</p>

  <h3>Plan ahead for installation</h3>
  <p>
    Reverse-cycle systems are relevant in both{" "}
    <a href="/seasonal/">winter and summer</a>, which creates demand at both ends of the year.
  </p>
  <p>
    Waiting until the first major cold spell or heatwave may reduce installer availability. Summer
    scheduling can become especially difficult when demand is high, and some trades and
    installation businesses operate with reduced capacity over the Christmas and New Year period.
  </p>
  <p>
    Planning earlier gives you more time to compare systems, confirm electrical requirements,
    inspect possible installation positions and obtain quotes. It does not guarantee a particular
    price or installation date, but it can reduce the pressure to accept the first available option.
  </p>

  <h2>Ducted heating</h2>
  <p>
    Ducted systems distribute conditioned air through ceiling or floor outlets and can suit
    households that want to heat several rooms from one central system.
  </p>
  <p>The main options include ducted reverse-cycle air conditioning and ducted gas heating.</p>
  <p>
    Ducted reverse-cycle systems provide both heating and cooling. Zoning can allow selected parts
    of the home to operate separately, although zoning design, return-air placement and minimum
    airflow requirements need professional consideration.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ducted gas heating provides effective whole-home heat but uses gas for combustion and electricity
    to operate fans. Existing owners should consider servicing requirements and the condition of the
    ductwork. Anyone planning a new system should compare it with reverse-cycle alternatives rather
    than assuming the existing fuel type remains the most suitable choice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ducted systems involve more than choosing an appliance. Roof or underfloor access, duct routes,
    insulation, zoning, switchboard capacity, outdoor-unit placement and installation quality all
    affect the result.
  </p>

  <h2>Other fixed heating options</h2>
  <p>
    Hydronic heating, electric underfloor heating, wood heaters and fixed gas heaters may suit
    particular homes, climates or renovation projects, but each introduces additional installation
    and maintenance considerations.
  </p>
  <p>
    Wood heaters require suitable flues, clearances, fuel storage and compliance with local
    restrictions. Gas appliances require correct ventilation, installation and servicing.
    Underfloor and hydronic systems make the most sense when considered as part of a renovation or
    new build rather than as a simple appliance replacement.
  </p>
  <p>
    Major fixed systems should be assessed with appropriately licensed professionals before products
    are ordered.
  </p>

  <h2>Australia does not have one heating climate</h2>
  <p>A heater that makes sense in one part of Australia may be unnecessary or inadequate in another.</p>

  <h3>Cooler and cold regions</h3>
  <p>
    Households that heat rooms for long periods may benefit more from fixed reverse-cycle systems,
    effective zoning and improvements to the building envelope. Insulation and window performance
    become especially important where there is a large difference between indoor and outdoor
    temperatures.
  </p>

  <h3>Temperate coastal regions</h3>
  <p>
    Room-by-room heating may be sufficient, particularly in smaller or well-insulated homes.
    Humidity, salt exposure and outdoor-unit positioning may still affect fixed-system choices.
  </p>

  <h3>Subtropical and tropical regions</h3>
  <p>
    Heating may only be needed during occasional cool mornings or evenings. A small portable heater
    or the heating mode of an existing reverse-cycle air conditioner may be more practical than a
    dedicated whole-home system.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cooling performance may be more important than heating performance when choosing a new air
    conditioner, but the local Zoned Energy Rating information should still be checked.
  </p>

  <h3>Inland and high-altitude areas</h3>
  <p>
    Large temperature changes, dry air and very cold nights can alter heating requirements. Check
    that any reverse-cycle system is designed to perform in the winter conditions expected at the
    property.
  </p>

  <h2>Improve the room before buying a bigger heater</h2>
  <p>A heater cannot compensate efficiently for unlimited heat loss.</p>
  <p>Before increasing heater size, inspect the room for:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>gaps around external doors</li>
    <li>worn door and window seals</li>
    <li>unsealed exhaust fans</li>
    <li>uncovered hard floors</li>
    <li>thin or poorly fitted window coverings</li>
    <li>open doorways to unused rooms</li>
    <li>missing or inadequate ceiling insulation</li>
    <li>large areas of unshaded or single-glazed glass</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Close off rooms that are not being heated. Open curtains when winter sun can warm the room, then
    close them before the temperature drops. Properly fitted curtains, blinds and pelmets can reduce
    heat loss through windows.
  </p>
  <p>
    Reversible ceiling fans can also help move warm air that has collected near the ceiling,
    provided they are operated in the appropriate winter direction and at a suitable low speed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Wall construction matters as well, but no single building material solves every heat-loss
    problem. Homes built with autoclaved aerated concrete, insulated concrete or other thermally
    performing wall systems can still lose heat through windows, ceilings, gaps and poorly
    controlled airflow.
  </p>
  <p>
    Consider the home as a complete system rather than judging comfort from one material or
    appliance.
  </p>

  <h2>Heater safety belongs in the buying decision</h2>
  <p>Safety features are useful, but they do not replace correct placement and supervision.</p>
  <p>Before using a heater:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>read and follow the manufacturer’s instructions</li>
    <li>inspect the plug, cord, body, grille and controls</li>
    <li>keep clothing, curtains, bedding and furniture at least one metre away</li>
    <li>place portable heaters on a stable surface</li>
    <li>keep them clear of walkways</li>
    <li>do not overload power points or power boards</li>
    <li>supervise children and pets around operating heaters</li>
    <li>turn heaters off before leaving the room or going to sleep</li>
    <li>maintain working smoke alarms in accordance with local requirements</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Do not use outdoor gas heaters, barbecues or other outdoor combustion products inside. They can
    produce carbon monoxide, which cannot be seen, tasted or smelled.
  </p>

  <h2>Before You Buy Checklist</h2>
  <p>Before choosing a heating option, check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>What are the room dimensions and ceiling height?</li>
    <li>Is the room enclosed or open to adjoining areas?</li>
    <li>How well is the ceiling insulated?</li>
    <li>Are there draughts around doors, windows or exhaust fans?</li>
    <li>How much glass does the room have?</li>
    <li>Is quick heat or steady background warmth more important?</li>
    <li>How many hours at a time will the heater operate?</li>
    <li>Is fan noise acceptable?</li>
    <li>Will children or pets use the room?</li>
    <li>Can the heater maintain safe clearances?</li>
    <li>Is there a suitable wall power point?</li>
    <li>Does the manufacturer permit the intended use?</li>
    <li>Does a fixed system require electrical or switchboard work?</li>
    <li>Has the relevant climate-zone rating been checked?</li>
    <li>Are installation quotes and trade availability confirmed?</li>
    <li>Would sealing draughts or improving window coverings solve part of the problem?</li>
    <li>Are filters, flues, ducts or other components likely to require ongoing servicing?</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Choosing for the home you actually have</h2>
  <p>The largest or most expensive heating system is not automatically the right one.</p>
  <p>
    A fan heater can be practical for short use in a small room. An oil column or panel heater may
    suit quiet background heating. Electric bedding can warm a person without heating the entire
    house. Reverse-cycle air conditioning can provide efficient heating and cooling where a fixed
    system makes sense.
  </p>
  <p>
    Start with the room, local climate and length of use. Then consider installation, running
    behaviour, safety and the amount of heat the home is losing.
  </p>
  <p>
    A well-matched heater in a prepared room will usually provide a better result than a larger
    appliance fighting draughts, open spaces and poor insulation.
  </p>

